    "result_text": "All 273 tests pass \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**, with no regressions.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change makes telephone numbers stored as digits-only, normalized before the uniqueness check.\n\n**The core obstacle:** `OwnerFieldsDto.telephone` carries `@Pattern(regexp = \"^[0-9]*$\")` (generated from `openapi.yml`) and is validated by `@Valid` in `BuildOwner`, so a formatted number like `(613) 555-0100` was rejected as a 400 before any normalization could run.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 relaxed the telephone `pattern` from `^[0-9]*$` to `^[0-9 ()-]*$` so formatted input passes bind-time validation (the DTO is regenerated at `generate-sources`).\n\n2. **`TelephoneNormalizer.java`** (new) \u2014 small helper stripping all non-digits (`replaceAll(\"\\\\D\", \"\")`), null-safe.\n\n3. **`BuildOwner.java`** \u2014 normalizes the built owner's telephone to digits-only immediately after mapping, i.e. *before* the `rejectDuplicate` step in the POST pipeline. So both the stored value (read back via GET) and the uniqueness comparison use the normalized form.\n\n4. **`ApplyOwner.java`** \u2014 normalizes on the PUT/update path too, keeping stored values consistently digits-only (and satisfying the `Owner` entity's `^[0-9]{10}$` pattern).\n\nBecause normalization happens in `BuildOwner` before `RejectDuplicateOwner`, `(613) 555-0100` and `6135550100` collapse to the same `6135550100` and correctly collide as a 409 \u2014 exactly what the two new `Cp11Tests` assert.",
